      <section environment="" chapter_type="" type="Section" wbtag="section" title="Quotient Rings - continued" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" num="1" serial="10.1">
        <title environment="title" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="title" scope="section" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" serial="" slide="">
          <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="title" wbtag="paragraphs">Quotient Rings - continued</paragraphs>
        </title>
        <slides environment="" chapter_type="">
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" canon_num="3" scope="section" id="s3" slide="3" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="" section_title="Quotient Rings - continued">
            <keywords slide="3" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078"/>
            <title environment="title" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="title" scope="section" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" serial="" slide="3">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="title" wbtag="paragraphs">Quotient Rings - continued</paragraphs>
            </title>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Example" wbtag="eg" num="1" md5="4f706ef40c63abeca5dda6b8259a25fa" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="3" item="10.1">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs"> Let $m$ be a natural number.
Consider the map $\phi : \mathbb{Z} \ra \ZZ_m$ defined by:
\[
\phi(n) = {n}_m, \quad \forall\, n \in \mathbb{Z},
\]
where ${n}_m$ is the remainder of the division of $n$ by $m$.</paragraphs>
              <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement"/>
              <strong xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" environment="statement">
                <paragraphs xmlns="http://www.math.cuhk.edu.hk/~pschan/cranach" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">Exercise:</paragraphs>
              </strong>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs"> $\phi$ is a homomorphism.</paragraphs>
              <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement"/>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs">It is clear that $\phi$ is surjective, and that $\ker \phi = m\ZZ$.
So, it follows from the First Isomorphism Theorem that:
\[
\ZZ_m \cong \ZZ/m\ZZ.
\] </paragraphs>
            </statement>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="4" id="s4" slide="4" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="" section_title="Quotient Rings - continued">
            <keywords slide="4" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078">
              <keyword chapter="10" slide="4">Gaussian Integers</keyword>
            </keywords>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Definition" wbtag="defn" num="2" title="Gaussian Integers" md5="6508d08d4c532fd4090349222529c9bc" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="4" item="10.2">
              <title environment="title" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="statement" scope="" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" serial="" slide="4">Gaussian Integers</title>
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs"> Let:
\[
\mathbb{Z}[i] = \{z \in \mathbb{C}: z = a + bi \text{ for some } a, b \in \mathbb{Z}\},
\]
where $i = \sqrt{-1}$. </paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Exercise" wbtag="ex" num="3" md5="934d5ab0532d22f000cda91b29e6c63e" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="4" item="10.3">
              <newcol course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="newcol">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> Show that the set $\mathbb{Z}[i]$ is a ring under the usual addition $+$
and multiplication $\times$ operations on $\mathbb{C}$.</paragraphs>
                <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">Moreover, we have $0_{\mathbb{Z}[i]} = 0$, $1_{\mathbb{Z}[i]} = 1$,
and:
\[
-(a + bi) = (-a) + (-b) i
\]
for any $a, b \in \mathbb{Z}$. </paragraphs>
              </newcol>
            </statement>
            <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment=""/>
          </slide>
          <slide environment="" chapter_type="Week" wbtag="slide" canon_num="5" data-lecture-skip="true" id="s5" slide="5" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" chapter_title="" section_title="Quotient Rings - continued">
            <keywords slide="5" environ="slide" chapter="10" course="Math 2078"/>
            <statement environment="statement" chapter_type="Week" type="Example" wbtag="eg" num="4" md5="a780d473975decbb35a820a29e106575" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="5" item="10.4">
              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="statement" wbtag="paragraphs"> The ring $\mathbb{Z}[i]/(1 + 3i)$ is isomorphic to $\mathbb{Z}/10\ZZ$. </paragraphs>
            </statement>
            <substatement environment="substatement" chapter_type="Week" type="Proof" wbtag="proof" md5="064e5b43d04f32f770220d6b3d3c85dd" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" slide="5" of="a780d473975decbb35a820a29e106575" of-src-course="Math 2078" of-src-chapter="10" of-src-slide="5" of-type="Example" of-item="10.4" of-src-filename="week10.xml" ofmd5="a780d473975decbb35a820a29e106575" of-serial="">
              <of-title hidden="true">Example 10.4</of-title>
              <newcol course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="newcol">
                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> Define a map $\phi: \ZZ \ra \ZZ[i]/(1 + 3i)$ as follows:
\[
\phi(n) = \ol{n},\quad \forall\, n \in \ZZ,
\]
where $\ol{n}$ is the residue of $n \in \ZZ[i]$ modulo $(1 + 3i)$.</paragraphs>
                <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> It is clear that $\phi$ is a homomorphism ( </paragraphs>
                  <strong xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" environment="newcol">
                    <paragraphs xmlns="http://www.math.cuhk.edu.hk/~pschan/cranach" course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">Exercise</paragraphs>
                  </strong>
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> ).</paragraphs>
                  <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs">Observe that in $\mathbb{Z}[i]$, we have:
\[
1 + 3i \equiv 0 \mod (1 + 3i),
\] </paragraphs>
                  <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                    <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> which implies that: </paragraphs>
                    <steps course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="steps" stepsid="steps1">
\begin{align*}
\class{steps}{\cssId{step0}{1}} &amp; \class{steps}{\cssId{step1}{\equiv -3i \mod (1 + 3i)}}
\\
\class{steps}{\cssId{step2}{i\cdot 1}} &amp; \class{steps}{\cssId{step3}{\equiv i \cdot (-3i) \mod (1 + 3i)}}
\\
\class{steps}{\cssId{step4}{i}} &amp; \class{steps}{\cssId{step5}{\equiv 3 \mod (1 + 3i).}}
\end{align*}
</steps>
                    <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                      <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> Hence, for all $a, b \in \ZZ$,
\[
\ol{a + bi} = \ol{a + 3b} = \phi(a + 3b)
\]
in $\ZZ[i]/(1 + 3i)$.  Hence, $\phi$ is surjective.</paragraphs>
                      <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                      <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                        <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> Suppose $n$ is an element of $\ZZ$ such that $\phi(n) = \ol{n} = 0$.
Then, by the definition of the quotient ring we have:
\[
n \in (1 + 3i).
\] </paragraphs>
                        <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                          <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> This means that there exist $a, b \in \ZZ$ such that:
\[
n = (a + bi)(1 + 3i) = (a - 3b) + (3a + b)i,
\] </paragraphs>
                          <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                            <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> which implies that $3a + b = 0$, or equivalently, $b = -3a$.  Hence:
\[
n = a - 3b = a - 3(-3a) = 10 a,
\] </paragraphs>
                            <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                              <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> which implies that $\ker \phi \subseteq 10\ZZ$.  Conversely, for all $m \in \ZZ$,
we have:
\[
\phi(10 m) = \ol{10 m} = \ol{(1 + 3i)(1 - 3i)m} = 0
\]
in $\ZZ[i]/(1 + 3i)$.</paragraphs>
                              <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                              <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                                <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> This shows that $10\ZZ \subseteq \ker \phi$.  Hence, $\ker \phi = 10\ZZ$.</paragraphs>
                                <newline course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                                <collapse course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="collapse">
                                  <paragraphs course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol" wbtag="paragraphs"> It now follows from the First Isomorphism Theorem that:
\[
\ZZ/10\ZZ \cong \ZZ[i]/(1 + 3i).
\] </paragraphs>
                                  <qed course="Math 2078" chapter="10" chapter_type="" section="1" subsection="" subsubsection="" environment="newcol"/>
                                </collapse>
                              </collapse>
                            </collapse>
                          </collapse>
                        </collapse>
                      </collapse>
                    </collapse>
                  </collapse>
                </collapse>
              </newcol>
            </substatement>
          </slide>
        </slides>
      </section>
